15. Usability and Accessibility Requirements
The interface must be easy to understand for employees with different levels of technical knowledge.

Use plain language and action-oriented labels.
Make buttons visually distinguishable from informational elements.
Ensure keyboard navigation and visible focus states.
Provide accessible names for icon-only buttons.
Maintain sufficient contrast between text and backgrounds.
Make forms understandable through clear labels and validation messages.
Provide loading, success, error, and empty states.
Confirm important actions, such as ticket submission and issue resolution.
Ensure responsive behaviour on desktop, tablet, and mobile.
Avoid relying exclusively on colour to communicate ticket status.
Make sources and suggested solutions easy to identify.
The interface should explain what an employee needs to do next without requiring them to understand NLP, RAG, embeddings, or LLM terminology.

16. Final Design Principle
The defining characteristic of PowerGrid Copilot should be its warm, minimal, conversation-first workspace.

The cream background, black rounded sidebar, white navigation pill, beige response panels, soft pastel indicators, and black pill-shaped action buttons should create a consistent visual identity across the application.

The AI Assistant should remain the centre of the employee experience. Supporting features should be easy to discover, while ticket management, knowledge retrieval, and chat history should follow the same visual language.

Every screen should answer three questions clearly:

Where am I in the application?
What information do I need here?
What should I do next?
The goal is not to make the application look more technical or more AI-powered. The goal is to make IT support feel clear, trustworthy, approachable, and effortless.